Fix ckb-librocksdb-sys cross-compilation platform detection.

ckb-librocksdb-sys's build.rs runs `rocksdb/build_tools/build_detect_platform`
(a bash script) to pick up Linux-specific defines (ROCKSDB_PLATFORM_POSIX,
OS_LINUX, ROCKSDB_FALLOCATE_PRESENT, ...) and guards the invocation with
`cfg!(target_os = "windows")`.

The problem: `cfg!()` evaluates against the BUILD host, not the Cargo TARGET.
When cross-compiling from Linux to any non-Linux target (MinGW Windows, MSVC
Windows, macOS Darwin), the check passes and the Linux platform script runs
anyway, polluting CFLAGS with Linux-only defines that then collide with the
target-specific defines the same build.rs adds later (`-DOS_WIN`, etc.).

Fix: use the Cargo `TARGET` env var instead of `cfg!()`.  Only actually run
the Linux detect script when the target is Linux; skip it for every other
platform.  This matches what the comment in build.rs always intended to do.

diff --git a/build.rs b/build.rs
index 2544aeb..31c8773 100644
--- a/build.rs
+++ b/build.rs
@@ -7,7 +7,7 @@ use std::path::PathBuf;
 use std::process::Command;

 fn get_flags_from_detect_platform_script() -> Option<Vec<String>> {
-    if !cfg!(target_os = "windows") {
+    if env::var("TARGET").unwrap_or_default().contains("linux") {
         let mut cmd = Command::new("bash");

         // if ROCKSDB_USE_IO_URING is not set, treat as enable
